# Documentation and content standards

Apply when writing or editing **prose intended for people** — documentation, guides, references, page and marketing copy, changelogs, release notes, site metadata. **Read the repo's own `AGENTS.md` / style guide / `CONTRIBUTING.md` first — its conventions outrank these defaults**, especially a named ground-truth source for domain facts.

**Out of scope: agent-instruction files** — `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.cursorrules`, `rules/*.mdc`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, and skill or agent definitions. Read them for conventions; do not rewrite them here. They address a model rather than a reader, so rules below invert: repetition is deliberate, hard constraints stay hard, and rigid parallel structure is a feature.

## The hard rule

**A claim that cannot be traced to a source does not ship.** Never originate statistics, benchmarks, user or customer counts, testimonials, named customers, awards, endorsements, urgency devices, or comparative superlatives. Hedging is not a fix — "up to", "designed to", "teams report" preserve the violation. Replace the invented outcome with the **observable mechanism**, which is also more persuasive to a technical reader:

> ❌ "Cuts review time by 40%."
> ✅ "Flags unsourced statistics, doc-kind bleed, and stale inventories."

State maturity plainly (`shipped` / `experimental` / `planned` / `deprecated`) and verify every count, component table, and feature list against the tree — inventories rot fastest. Domain facts come from the repo's named ground-truth source, never from memory.

## One document kind per file

Decide before the first sentence: **tutorial** (one path, no branches, works verbatim), **how-to** (one goal, assumes competence), **reference** (exhaustive, indicative, mirrors the code, no narrative), **explanation** (context and trade-offs — the only kind that may argue). Most bad documentation is two kinds in one file. If the answer is "both", that is two documents.

## Style essentials

Answer first — the conclusion, command, or definition opens each section; background never precedes it. Active voice, present tense, one idea per sentence. Cut "simply", "just", "obviously", "very", "seamlessly", "powerful", "robust". Sentence-case headings, one `h1`, language-tagged fences with no `$` prompt. Link the noun, never "click here". Cross-reference rather than duplicate — one home per fact. Sections must stand alone: a reader arriving at an `h2` acts without reading upward (this also determines whether AI retrieval can use the chunk).

## Run the tools

Whatever `vale` and `markdownlint` enforce, run them rather than reasoning it out. The repo's own `.vale.ini` / `.markdownlint*` win; `/docs-lint-setup` scaffolds them if absent.

